following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 4y &=6 \\\\ - 2x - y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186074059 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 4y &=6 \\\\ - 2x - y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186074060 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 2x - y =-2, l_{2}: 6x - 3y =-12$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186074060 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{alignedat}{2}\nl_{1}:\\quad & 2x - y &{}={}& -2 \\\\[6pt]\nl_{2}:\\quad & 6x - 3y &{}={}& -12\n\\end{alignedat}\n$$\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186074086 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y - 3x - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4y - 12x - 8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186074086 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y - 3x - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4y - 12x - 8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186074087 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 5y &=15 \\\\ 4x - 10y &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186074087 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 5y &=15 \\\\ 4x - 10y &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186074089 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = 3x - 4, l_{2}: 12x - 4y = 16$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186074089 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{alignedat}{2}\nl_{1}:\\quad & y &{}={}& 3x - 4 \\\\[6pt]\nl_{2}:\\quad & 12x - 4y &{}={}& 16\n\\end{alignedat}\n$$\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186074138 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 3y &= -6 \\\\[6pt]\n3x - y &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186074138 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the lines $9x - 3y =-6$ and $3x - y =-2$." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186075049 1064 "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a_{1}x + b_{1}y &=c_{1} \\\\ a_{2}x + b_{2}y &=c_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines.\n\n  ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png)\n\n  Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point.\n\n  ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png)\n\n  Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide.\n\n  ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png)\n\n  Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186075049 1064 "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a_{1}x + b_{1}y &=c_{1} \\\\ a_{2}x + b_{2}y &=c_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n| ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png) | ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png) | ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines. Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point. Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide. Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186075062 1064 "When a system of equations has one solution, we can describe the solution as a single point $(x,y)$. This represents the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nNow, suppose a system of equations has *infinitely many* solutions. Can we describe the solutions to the system in a similar way?\n\nIndeed, we can. To demonstrate, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that this system has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the corresponding lines coincide.\n\nOur goal is to express *all* the solutions of this system in the form $({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}})$.\n\nTo do this, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the equations for $y$. It doesn't matter which, so let's pick the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= 3x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any given $x$-value, the corresponding $y$-value is\n\n$$\ny = 3x+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the intersection points as\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}}) = \\left({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{3x+2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x$ is *any* number." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186075062 1064 "When a system of equations has one solution, we can describe the solution as a single point $(x,y)$. This represents the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nNow, suppose a system of equations has *infinitely many* solutions. Can we describe the solutions to the system in a similar way?\n\nIndeed, we can. To demonstrate, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that this system has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the corresponding lines coincide.\n\nOur goal is to express *all* the solutions of this system in the form $({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}})$. To do this, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the equations for $y$. It doesn't matter which, so let's pick the first equation.\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2⇒y = 3x + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any given $x$-value, the corresponding $y$-value is\n\n$$\ny = 3x+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the intersection points as\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}}) = \\left({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{3x+2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x$ is *any* number." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186075063 1064 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo show that this system has infinitely many solutions, we solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{alignedat}{2}3x-y &=-2 &\\quad& \\mid\\cdot(-2) \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{alignedat}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n6x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always* true. This implies that this system of equations has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3x - y =-2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6x -2y=-4}\n$$\n\nboth represent the *same straight line*. In other words, the two lines **coincide**.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e98008aa5972889efdd3af0689da310c69bca06dd410a391a37e22aa8b0098.png)\n\nThese two lines intersect at an infinite number of points. The system has infinitely many solutions because there are infinitely many intersection points.\n\nAnother way to see that this system has infinitely many solutions is to notice that the lines are nonzero multiples of each other.\n\nFor example, if we multiply the equation of the first line by $2$, we get the second line!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 \\\\ 2\\cdot(3x-y) &=2\\cdot(-2) \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a system with *no solutions*." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186075063 1064 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo show that this system has infinitely many solutions, we solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n6x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always* true. This implies that this system of equations has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3x - y =-2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6x -2y=-4}\n$$\n\nboth represent the *same straight line*. In other words, the two lines **coincide**.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e98008aa5972889efdd3af0689da310c69bca06dd410a391a37e22aa8b0098.png)\n\nThese two lines intersect at an infinite number of points. The system has infinitely many solutions because there are infinitely many intersection points.\n\nAnother way to see that this system has infinitely many solutions is to notice that the lines are nonzero multiples of each other.\n\nFor example, if we multiply the equation of the first line by $2$, we get the second line!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 \\\\ 2\\cdot(3x-y) &=2\\cdot(-2) \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a system with *no solutions*." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186075064 1064 "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  (x, y) &= (0, 3(0) + 2) \\\\\n         &= (0, 2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n  This point is indeed a solution to the system:\n\n  $$\n  \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\n  3(0) - 2 &= -2 \\\\\n  6(0) - 2(2) &= -4\n  \\end{aligned}\\right.\n  $$\n\n  Both equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  (x, y) &= (1, 3(1) + 2) \\\\\n         &= (1, 5).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n  This point is another solution to the system:\n\n  $$\n  \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\n  3(1) - 5 &= -2 \\\\\n  6(1) - 2(5) &= -4\n  \\end{aligned}\\right.\n  $$\n\n  Both equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186075064 1064 "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (0, 3(0) + 2) = (0, 2)$.\nThis point is indeed a solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(0) - 2 &=-2 \\\\ 6(0) - 2(2) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (1, 3(1) + 2) = (1, 5)$.\nThis point is another solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(1) - 5 &=-2 \\\\ 6(1) - 2(5) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186542434 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + y &= 8 \\\\[6pt]\n-8x - 2y &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186542434 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the lines $4x + y = 8$ and $-8x - 2y =-16$." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186542435 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n-4x + 16y &= -12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186542435 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the lines $x - 4y = 3$ and $-4x + 16y =-12$." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186542442 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 10 \\\\[6pt]\n3x - 6y &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186542442 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the lines $x - 2y = 10$ and $3x - 6y = 30$." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186542448 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n-4x + 2y &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186542448 1071 "Find all points of intersection of the lines $2x - y = 1$ and $-4x + 2y =-2$." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186542452 1071 "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\[6pt]\n6x - 3y &= -18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpress your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are\n\n$(t,$ {{field-1}} $),$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number." 13194139536555 17592186045436 true] [17592186542452 1071 "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $2x - y =-6$ and $6x - 3y =-18$. Express your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(t$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $t$ is any number." 13194139536555 17592186045436 false] [17592186542462 1071 "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= -11 \\\\[6pt]\n8x - 4y